jdg00@ccc.amdahl.com (Josh Grosse) writes:

>Wouldn't it be nice if one of the Nethack options was a random number
>seed?  This way if two people wanted to compete for points playing the
>exact same characters in the exact same dungeion

That's an excellent idea.  A friend of mine and I had the idea that we could
write a random-adventure type game (it was going to be a text game, but
that's irrelevant) and use seeds in an orderly manner to generate levels.

What's amazing about this idea is that the save files are TINY.  You need
only to save a seed, plus the status of anything the player has affected.

The problem with working this into nethack, it would seem, is that the
number of times the random number generator is called in between generating
two levels is _itself_ random, so that great weapon in the shop on level
8 might not come to pass if you tried opening the chest on level 1!

The game could undoubtedly be redesigned to work around that.  For instance,
you could take the starting seed, then generate one number to get the seed
for generating level one.  Then when it's time to make level two, go back
and reseed to the starting seed, then generate two numbers and seed level
two with the second, and so on.

In that way, you could ensure that if the starting seed were the same, the
player's starting inventory and the initial contents of each dungeon level
would be the same.  Of course, monster appearances would still be random,
which is probably okay.  By using drand48, you still have a HUGE number of
games possible, and of course, it could be possible to turn the feature
off, to allow the game to be as random as it always has been.

